+                The original Found‑Rᵧ was an idea rather than an application: tools for making tools. Prompts
+                that became GPTs that wrote better prompts, which produced better GPTs. That loop worked. It
+                produced the prototypes and the working method that made
+                <a href="/projects/glee-fully-chai-chasers/">Glee-fully Chai Chasers</a> possible.
+              </p>
+              <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1.25rem;line-height:1.8;">
+                What it could not do was package anything. The prompts, the scripts, and the proto-GPTs stayed
+                trapped as clever artifacts in a private workspace, reusable only by the person who wrote them
+                and only while he still remembered how. The gap was never the thinking. It was the absence of a
+                way to turn the thinking into something with an interface, a repository, and a URL.
+              </p>
+              <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1.5rem;line-height:1.8;">
+                That gap is now closed. The page you are reading sits in front of the workbench that the
+                original idea was always describing.
+              </p>
